UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Fuel is one of the biggest running costs of owning a car, and small changes in how you drive and look after your vehicle can add up to real savings over a year. You do not need a new car or expensive equipment to use less fuel. Most of the savings come from simple habits, regular checks, and planning your trips a little better. This guide covers fifteen proven ways to cut your fuel use, starting with the ones that cost nothing at all.</p><h2>Start with how you drive</h2><p>Your driving style has the biggest effect on fuel consumption. Smooth, calm driving uses far less fuel than hard acceleration and heavy braking. When you accelerate gently, the engine works less hard and burns less fuel. When you look ahead and slow down early for traffic lights, junctions, and corners, you avoid wasting the energy you have already built up. Keeping a steady speed also helps, especially on longer journeys where constant speeding up and slowing down adds up quickly. Try to keep your speed moderate and consistent, and give yourself enough time so you are not rushing. A relaxed driver almost always uses less fuel than a hurried one.</p><h2>Fifteen ways to use less fuel</h2><ul><li><strong>Accelerate gently.</strong> Press the accelerator smoothly and build speed gradually instead of pushing the pedal hard from a standstill. Gentle acceleration is one of the easiest ways to save fuel.</li><li><strong>Look ahead and brake early.</strong> Watch the road ahead, ease off the accelerator early, and let the car slow down naturally before you need the brakes. This keeps momentum and saves fuel.</li><li><strong>Keep a steady speed.</strong> Avoid repeated speeding up and slowing down. On open roads, a steady pace uses less fuel than constantly changing speed.</li><li><strong>Use cruise control on long trips.</strong> Where your car has cruise control and the road is clear, it can help hold a steady speed and reduce the small speed changes that waste fuel.</li><li><strong>Slow down a little.</strong> Higher speeds use much more fuel, and the difference between a fast cruise and a moderate one can be significant over a long journey. Arriving a few minutes later often costs very little time.</li><li><strong>Remove unnecessary weight.</strong> Clear out heavy items you do not need from the boot and cabin. Extra weight makes the engine work harder, especially in stop-start driving.</li><li><strong>Take off roof racks and boxes when not in use.</strong> Roof loads increase wind resistance and raise fuel use, even when they are empty. Remove them between trips.</li><li><strong>Check tyre pressure regularly.</strong> Under-inflated tyres increase rolling resistance and make the engine work harder. Check pressures at least once a month and before long trips, using the figures in your owner manual or on the door sticker.</li><li><strong>Keep your car serviced.</strong> Follow the service schedule in your owner manual. Clean filters, fresh oil of the correct grade, and a healthy engine all help the car run efficiently.</li><li><strong>Fix warning lights promptly.</strong> A warning light can signal a fault that makes the engine use more fuel. Do not ignore it, get the car checked by a qualified mechanic.</li><li><strong>Plan your trips.</strong> Combine several errands into one journey instead of making many short trips. A warm engine runs more efficiently than a cold one, so fewer cold starts save fuel.</li><li><strong>Avoid idling for long periods.</strong> If you are stopped for more than a minute or so and it is safe to do so, switching the engine off usually saves more fuel than letting it idle.</li><li><strong>Use air conditioning wisely.</strong> Air conditioning puts extra load on the engine. At low speeds, opening a window can be more efficient, at higher speeds, closed windows with air conditioning are usually better.</li><li><strong>Choose the right gear.</strong> In a manual car, change up early and avoid revving the engine hard in low gears. In an automatic, a gentle right foot lets the gearbox choose efficient gears.</li><li><strong>Track your fuel use.</strong> Note your mileage and fuel fill-ups so you can see what works. A simple record helps you spot changes and keeps the savings visible.</li></ul><h2>What to check each month</h2><p>A short monthly routine keeps the savings going. Check tyre pressures, look for uneven tyre wear, and make sure nothing is dragging or rubbing underneath the car. Check that the air filter is not blocked and that fluid levels are correct. Keep a note of your fuel consumption so you can see whether it is improving. If consumption suddenly gets worse without an obvious reason, such as a change in driving or weather, have the car inspected, because a developing fault can waste fuel long before it causes a breakdown.</p><h2>Small savings add up</h2><p>No single tip here will transform your fuel bill overnight, but together they make a real difference. Start with the free habits, such as smooth driving, steady speed, and removing extra weight, then add the monthly checks. Over a year of driving, the savings can pay for a service, a set of tyres, or simply stay in your pocket. Drive calmly, keep the car healthy, and let the small savings add up.</p>' WHERE id = 6;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Modern cars are fitted with driver assistance systems, known together as ADAS, which stands for Advanced Driver Assistance Systems. These systems use cameras, radar, and sensors to watch the road, warn the driver about hazards, and in some situations take brief action, such as braking or steering. They can make driving safer and less tiring, but they are not self-driving systems. The driver stays responsible at all times. This guide explains the main systems, what they can do, and what they cannot do.</p><h2>What ADAS is</h2><p>ADAS is a group of features that help the driver rather than replace the driver. A camera behind the windscreen, radar units in the bumpers, and sometimes ultrasonic sensors around the car feed information to a computer. The computer compares what it sees with patterns it has been designed to recognise, such as lane markings, other vehicles, and pedestrians. When it detects a risk, it can warn the driver with a sound, a light, or a vibration, and in some cases it can act on its own for a short moment. Different cars have different combinations of these features, and the exact names vary by manufacturer, so always check your owner manual for what your car has and how it works.</p><h2>Lane keeping and lane departure warning</h2><p>Lane systems watch the road markings ahead of the car. A lane departure warning alerts you if the car starts to drift out of its lane without the indicator being used. A lane keeping system goes a step further and gently steers the car back toward the centre of the lane. These systems work best on well-marked roads in good weather. They can struggle with faded markings, roadworks, heavy rain, snow, or sharp bends. They are designed to assist a driver who is paying attention, not to steer the car on their own, and they will usually switch off or warn you if you take your hands off the wheel for too long.</p><h2>Automatic emergency braking</h2><p>Automatic emergency braking, often called AEB, watches the road ahead for vehicles, pedestrians, and sometimes cyclists. If it judges that a collision is likely and the driver has not reacted, it first warns the driver and then applies the brakes automatically to reduce the impact or avoid it altogether. This system has prevented many rear-end collisions, especially in town traffic. However, it has limits. It may not react in time at very high speeds, it can miss objects that are partly hidden, and heavy rain, fog, or a dirty sensor can reduce its ability to see. It is a safety net, not a replacement for keeping a safe distance and watching the road.</p><h2>Adaptive cruise control</h2><p>Adaptive cruise control builds on ordinary cruise control by keeping a set distance from the vehicle ahead. You choose a cruising speed, and the system slows down when traffic ahead slows, then speeds up again when the road clears. Some versions can bring the car to a complete stop in a queue and move off again. This makes motorway and dual-carriageway driving much less tiring. It does not handle every situation well: it can be slow to react to a vehicle cutting in closely, it may not recognise stationary vehicles at speed, and it is not designed for sharp bends or town driving with many junctions.</p><h2>Other common systems</h2><p>Many cars also have blind spot monitoring, which warns you about a vehicle in the lane beside you, and rear cross traffic alert, which warns of traffic approaching from the side when you reverse out of a parking space. Parking assistance can steer the car into a space while you control the pedals, and a surround-view camera can show the area around the car. Each of these helps in a specific situation, and none of them removes the need for mirrors, shoulder checks, and attention.</p><h2>What ADAS cannot do</h2><p>It is important to understand the limits. ADAS cannot see everything: sensors can be blocked by dirt, snow, or ice, cameras can be dazzled by low sun, and radar can struggle in very heavy rain. The systems are designed for normal road situations and can be confused by unusual ones, such as roadworks, temporary markings, or a vehicle behaving unexpectedly. They do not make the car self-driving, and they do not take legal responsibility for driving. If a system fails or is switched off, the car simply becomes an ordinary car, and the driver must be ready to take over instantly. Never rely on a system to cover for distraction, tiredness, or driving too fast for the conditions.</p><h2>Using ADAS well</h2><p>To get the most from these systems, learn what your car has before you need it. Read the owner manual, try each feature on a quiet road, and know how to switch each one on and off. Keep the windscreen, cameras, and sensors clean, and have them checked after any accident or windscreen replacement, because a camera that is out of alignment may not work correctly. Treat warnings as useful information, not as annoyances to ignore. Above all, keep your hands on the wheel, your eyes on the road, and your attention on driving. ADAS works best as a careful assistant to an alert driver, and that partnership is what makes the road safer.</p>' WHERE id = 7;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Electric vehicle range figures can be confusing. A car may be advertised with one number, owners may report a different one, and winter driving can change the result again. None of this means the figures are dishonest, it means range depends on how and where the car is driven. This guide explains the main range numbers, why real-world range differs, how cold weather affects it, and how to read the figures before you buy.</p><h2>What WLTP range means</h2><p>Most electric cars sold in many markets quote a WLTP range figure. WLTP stands for Worldwide Harmonised Light Vehicles Test Procedure, a standard laboratory test used so that different cars can be compared fairly. The test runs the car through a set pattern of speeds, stops, and starts, at a controlled temperature, with no air conditioning or heating running and no extra load. Because every car is tested in the same way, the WLTP figure is useful for comparing one model with another. It is not a promise of what you will get on your own roads. Think of it as a standard benchmark, not a guarantee of daily range.</p><h2>Why real-world range is different</h2><p>Real driving is never as controlled as a laboratory test. Speed is the biggest factor: driving fast uses much more energy, because wind resistance rises sharply as speed increases. A long motorway journey at high speed will usually give less range than the same distance driven at moderate speeds. Short trips in town, with lots of stopping and starting, can be more efficient, because electric cars recover some energy when they slow down. Hills, headwinds, extra passengers, luggage, and roof loads all reduce range. Heating and air conditioning also use energy from the same battery that drives the car. Your own driving style matters too: smooth acceleration and gentle braking help, while hard acceleration wastes energy.</p><h2>Winter range and cold weather</h2><p>Range usually drops in cold weather, and the drop can be noticeable. Batteries work less efficiently when cold, and the car uses energy to warm the battery and the cabin. Short trips suffer most, because the car spends a large share of its energy heating up each time. Preheating the car while it is still plugged in can help, because the energy for warming comes from the charger rather than the battery. Using heated seats and a heated steering wheel, where fitted, can be more efficient than heating the whole cabin. Parking in a garage, if you have one, keeps the battery a little warmer. If you live somewhere with cold winters, expect a lower range in winter and plan charging around it.</p><h2>How to read the numbers before buying</h2><p>When you compare electric cars, start with the WLTP figure but do not stop there. Look for independent real-world range tests from motoring publications, which drive cars on normal roads in normal conditions and report what they actually achieved. Pay attention to the conditions of any test you read: the speed, the temperature, and whether heating or air conditioning was used. Think about your own driving. If most of your journeys are short town trips, you may find real range close to or even better than the official figure. If you regularly drive long distances at high speed, expect less. Consider the battery size as well as the range figure, and check how fast the car can charge, because a car that charges quickly can be more practical on long trips than one with a slightly longer range but slow charging.</p><h2>Planning charging around range</h2><p>Most electric car owners charge at home overnight and start each day with a full battery, so the quoted range matters less than it seems. The range matters most on long journeys, where you need to plan charging stops. A useful habit is to plan stops before you set off, using the car navigation or a charging app, and to keep a comfortable margin rather than running the battery very low. Charging speed slows as the battery fills up, so on a long trip it is often quicker to charge to around four fifths full and stop again later than to wait for a full charge. Knowing where chargers are along your route, and having a backup option, takes the stress out of a long journey.</p><h2>Questions worth asking</h2><p>Before you buy, ask a few practical questions. What range do owners report in conditions like yours? How much does the range drop in winter? How long does a typical fast charge take? Is there a heat pump fitted, which can reduce the energy used for heating in cold weather? Can you try the car for a day and drive your normal routes? A test drive on your own roads, in your normal driving style, tells you more than any brochure figure. Range is only one part of choosing an electric car, but understanding what the numbers really mean helps you choose a car that fits your life, not just the test laboratory.</p>' WHERE id = 8;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Cars make many sounds, and most of them are normal: the hum of tyres, the sound of the engine working, the click of indicators. But some noises are warnings. Learning to recognise the common ones can help you catch a small problem before it becomes an expensive repair, and in some cases it can keep you safe. This guide describes the noises drivers most often report, what they usually mean, and when to stop driving and get help.</p><h2>How to listen to your car</h2><p>Start by paying attention to when a noise happens. Does it appear when you start the engine, when you accelerate, when you brake, when you turn, or when you drive over bumps? Does it change with speed, or with engine speed? Is it coming from the front, the back, or underneath? These details help a mechanic find the cause much faster. If a new noise appears, make a note of when it started and what you were doing. If you are unsure about any noise, it is always better to have the car checked than to keep driving and hope it goes away.</p><h2>Knocking sounds</h2><p>A knocking sound from the engine is one of the noises to take most seriously. It can be a deep, rhythmic knock that changes with engine speed, and it may point to a problem inside the engine, such as worn bearings or a lack of oil pressure. A lighter knocking or pinging sound under acceleration can be caused by poor quality fuel or a timing problem. A knock that appears when you drive over bumps is more likely to come from the suspension or steering, such as a worn joint or a loose component. Engine knocking should never be ignored. Check the oil level if you know how to do so safely, and if the knock is loud, or the oil warning light is on, stop driving and seek a qualified mechanic.</p><h2>Squeals and squeaks</h2><p>A high-pitched squeal when you start the engine or accelerate often comes from a drive belt that is worn, loose, or glazed. The noise may fade as the engine warms up, but the belt still needs attention, because a failed belt can stop the car or damage the engine. A squeal when you brake usually means the brake pads are worn and a built-in wear indicator is touching the disc. This is a warning that the pads need replacing soon. A squeak from the suspension when you go over bumps can be a dry joint or a worn bush. Brake noises deserve prompt attention, because worn brakes take longer to stop the car.</p><h2>Rattles</h2><p>A rattle can be hard to trace because sound travels through the body of the car. A rattle from underneath, especially when the engine is idling, can be a loose heat shield or a damaged exhaust component. A rattle that appears at a certain engine speed and disappears at others is often something loose vibrating at that speed. A rattle from the engine area that gets louder with speed should be checked promptly, because it can be a loose component that may cause further damage if it comes free.</p><h2>Hisses, whistles, and grinding</h2><p>A hissing sound can mean a leak. A hiss from under the bonnet may be a vacuum hose or a coolant hose leaking, and a hiss from a tyre is usually air escaping from a puncture. A whistling sound that rises with speed can be a gap in a door seal or a window not fully closed, but a whistle from the engine can be an air leak in the intake system. A grinding sound is almost always serious. Grinding when you brake means the brake pads may be completely worn and metal is touching metal. Grinding when you change gear can mean a clutch problem. If you hear grinding from the brakes or the engine, stop driving as soon as it is safe and get the car checked.</p><h2>When to stop driving</h2><p>Some noises mean you should stop driving right away. Stop as soon as it is safe if you hear a loud knocking from the engine, grinding from the brakes, or a sudden loud bang. Stop too if a noise comes with a warning light, smoke, a smell of burning, leaking fluid, or a sudden change in how the car handles or brakes. Pull over in a safe place, switch the engine off, and do not try to keep driving to reach a garage. Call for roadside assistance or a qualified mechanic. It is far better to pay for a tow than to risk a breakdown in traffic or a repair bill that is much larger because a damaged part was driven further.</p><h2>Getting the problem fixed</h2><p>When you visit a mechanic, describe the noise as clearly as you can: when it happens, where it seems to come from, and whether it is getting worse. Ask what the cause is, what needs to be replaced, and whether it is safe to drive until the repair is done. Keep a record of repairs so you can spot patterns over time. Most noises have a simple cause and a straightforward fix when caught early, and the habit of listening to your car is one of the cheapest forms of maintenance there is.</p>' WHERE id = 9;
UPDATE articles SET body = '<p>Choosing between a new car and a used car is one of the biggest decisions a buyer faces. There is no single right answer. A new car offers a warranty and the latest features, while a used car usually costs much less and may offer better value. The best choice depends on your budget, how long you plan to keep the car, how much you drive, and how comfortable you are with a little uncertainty. This guide walks through the real trade-offs so you can decide with confidence.</p><h2>Depreciation: the biggest difference</h2><p>Depreciation is the fall in a car value over time, and it is the largest cost of owning a new car. A new car loses a large part of its value in the first few years, with the steepest drop usually in the first year. A buyer of a nearly new car lets the first owner absorb that early loss. This is why a two or three year old car can offer much of the usefulness of a new one at a far lower price. When you compare cars, think about the total cost over the years you plan to keep it, not just the price on the day you buy.</p><h2>Warranty and peace of mind</h2><p>A new car comes with a manufacturer warranty that covers most repairs for a set period or mileage, and that peace of mind is worth a lot to many buyers. If something goes wrong, the dealer fixes it. Some used cars still have part of their original warranty left, and some dealers offer their own warranty on used cars, but the cover is usually shorter and may exclude more items. An older car bought privately usually has no warranty at all, so any repair comes out of your pocket. Before you buy a used car, check exactly what warranty, if any, is included, what it covers, and for how long. A warranty does not remove the need for an inspection, it only limits what you pay if a covered fault appears.</p><h2>Inspection: never skip it on a used car</h2><p>A professional inspection is the most important step when buying used. A qualified mechanic can check the engine, brakes, suspension, tyres, and electrics, look for signs of accident damage or rust, and read the service history. Ask to see the service records, the ownership documents, and any receipts for past work. Check that the mileage is consistent with the car condition and its history. Take the car for a proper test drive on different kinds of road, and listen for unusual noises, check that the steering feels right, and make sure all the controls work. If the seller will not allow an inspection or a test drive, walk away. The cost of an inspection is small compared with the cost of buying a car with a hidden fault.</p><h2>Finance and running costs</h2><p>How you pay matters as much as what you pay. New cars are often sold with finance deals from the manufacturer, sometimes with low interest rates or deposit contributions, but read the terms carefully and work out the total amount you will pay over the whole agreement, not just the monthly figure. Used cars can be bought with a loan, through dealer finance, or with cash, and interest rates vary, so compare offers. Also compare running costs: insurance, fuel or electricity, road tax where it applies, and servicing. Set a total budget that includes the purchase price, insurance, and a reserve for maintenance, and do not stretch beyond it for a car you do not need.</p><h2>New car advantages</h2><p>Buying new has clear benefits. You choose the exact specification, colour, and options. The car has no previous owner and no hidden history. You get the full manufacturer warranty, the latest safety features, and often the newest fuel economy or electric range. The disadvantages are the price, the rapid early depreciation, and the fact that you may wait weeks or months for delivery of a car built to order. A new car suits buyers who plan to keep it for many years, value peace of mind, and can afford the higher upfront cost.</p><h2>Used car advantages</h2><p>Buying used has its own strengths. The price is lower, depreciation is slower, and the same budget buys a larger or better equipped car. The risks are that the car may have hidden faults, a patchy service history, or worn parts that need replacing soon. These risks can be reduced with a careful inspection, a check of the car history, and buying from a reputable seller. A used car suits buyers who want the best value, are happy to have a car checked by a mechanic, and prefer to keep the purchase price down.</p><h2>Making your decision</h2><p>Start by writing down your budget, your must-have features, and how long you plan to keep the car. Work out the total cost of each option over that period, including depreciation, finance, insurance, fuel, and likely maintenance. If you choose used, get a professional inspection before you pay anything, and check the documents carefully. If you choose new, compare the warranty and finance terms and make sure the total cost fits your budget. Whichever you choose, take your time and ask questions. A careful decision now saves money and worry for years.</p>' WHERE id = 10;
